Body:
Heads-up on the empty-reasoning filter in this PR — the `providerOptions != null` check is too loose and still lets the 400 through in one case:

```ts
if (part.type === "reasoning") {
  return part.text !== "" || part.providerOptions != null
}
```

With Anthropic **interleaved thinking** across several tool-call rounds, a turn that is cut off (e.g. output-length / max-tokens) can leave trailing reasoning parts that have **empty text and `providerOptions: {}`** (an empty object — still `!= null`). Those get kept and re-sent as malformed thinking blocks, and Anthropic rejects the *next* turn with the very error this PR targets:

```
messages.N.content.M: `thinking` or `redacted_thinking` blocks in the latest assistant message cannot be modified.
```

We hit this in production after applying an equivalent of this filter: the error recurred on a long Opus extended-thinking + multi-tool session. Tightening the predicate to require an actual signature/redactedData fixed it:

```ts
if (part.type === "reasoning") {
  if (part.text !== "") return true
  const po = part.providerOptions ?? {}
  // keep ONLY genuine redacted_thinking / signature-only blocks
  return Object.values(po).some((v) => v != null && (v.signature != null || v.redactedData != null))
}
```

Empty reasoning with `{}` / no signature is a truncated remnant and should be dropped, not replayed. Might be worth folding into this PR. (Related: #30046 fixes the upstream `message-v2.ts` strip that drops the signature on `differentModel`.)

Adding a concrete use case here because this issue tracks the broader MCP client capability gap. I do not want to create duplicate issues; I plan to keep the detailed write-up in the main elicitation issue (#23066), but I think this use case is also relevant to this umbrella tracker. If maintainers prefer a separate issue, I can open one and link it from both places.

Important context: the MCP server below, `Unity MCP Gateway`, is not a published public tool yet. It is currently an internal/private tool in my workflow, so this is not a runnable public reproduction. I am sharing the implementation shape because it depends on the exact MCP client capabilities discussed here.

The Gateway uses [CanonicalPath](https://github.com/romanilyin/canonicalpath), a cross-language, cross-platform path canonicalization library, so it can ask the user to approve a canonicalized, scoped operation rather than raw/ambiguous path input.

The relevant capability gap is `elicitation/create` nested inside `tools/call`.

My internal MCP server exposes a wrapper-style tool surface like:

~~~text
sgg_unity_call(project, command, args_json, mode)
~~~

A broad pre-call prompt for the wrapper tool is not enough, because the Gateway only knows whether a command needs human approval after it has:

~~~text
- parsed the command-specific args;
- resolved the Unity project;
- canonicalized path inputs through CanonicalPath/CanonicalFs;
- rejected traversal/symlink/reparse/scope escapes;
- evaluated policy as allow/ask/deny;
- computed the canonical scope summary;
- computed an args_hash;
- created a one-shot pending approval request.
~~~

The desired flow is:

~~~text
OpenCode calls tools/call
→ MCP server validates/canonicalizes/evaluates policy
→ MCP server sends elicitation/create
→ OpenCode renders a human approve/decline/cancel prompt
→ MCP server receives accept/decline/cancel
→ original tool call continues once or fails cleanly
~~~

For MCP `2025-06-18`, the client should advertise:

~~~json
{
  "capabilities": {
    "elicitation": {}
  }
}
~~~

The Gateway-side implementation already has this shape for capable clients:

~~~text
- official SDK transports where possible;
- `extra.sendRequest` used for server-to-client elicitation;
- boolean-only `approve` field;
- `accept + approve: true` creates a one-shot approval and retries the original call once;
- `decline`, `cancel`, error, or unsupported elicitation returns the existing confirmation_required fallback;
- no model-callable approve tool is exposed.
~~~

This use case also touches the Streamable HTTP/session part of this issue: capable clients should preserve the MCP session and support nested server-to-client requests during `tools/call`. Compatibility paths that call `tools/list` / `tools/call` without session support can still work for ordinary tools, but they cannot provide this native prompt UX.

The key requirement from this use case: OpenCode should support real MCP elicitation semantics rather than mapping this to a model-callable `question` tool or to coarse pre-call tool approval.

References:

- Main elicitation issue: #23066
- MCP elicitation spec: https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2025-06-18/client/elicitation
- CanonicalPath: https://github.com/romanilyin/canonicalpath
